<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mithridate (play)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1673">1673</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mithridate is a tragedy in five acts by the French playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-racine">Jean Racine</a>, first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in January 1673. It portrays the last days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and his rivalry with his sons Xiphares and Pharnace for the love of Monime. It was one of Racine's most popular plays and inspired Mozart's opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitridate-re-di-ponto">Mitridate, re di Ponto</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors Ariobarzanes, <b>Mithridates</b> II and Mithridates III extended their control towards the coast and married into the Seleucid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
